(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["interpretation-of-medical-literature-book-questions-chapter-10-prevention-quiz"]={"title":"Chapter 10: Prevention Quiz — Book Questions — Interpretation of Medical Literature","category":"Interpretation of Medical Literature","exam":"Book Questions","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"In a randomized controlled trial of screening chest x-rays and sputum cytology for lung cancer, approximately 9,000 men were randomized to screening for 6 years or a control group. After 20 years, the lung cancer mortality was the same in both groups (4.4/1,000 person-years in the screened group and 3.9/1,000 person-years in the control group). However, the median survival for patients diagnosed with lung cancer was 1.3 years in the screened group and 0.9 years in the control group. Also, screening found more lung cancer—206 cancers were diagnosed in the screened group and 160 in the control group.\n\nWhat bias is the best explanation for the improved survival in the face of no improvement in the mortality rate in this study?","choices":["Lead-time bias","Survival bias","Compliance bias","Length-time bias"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Lead time is the interval between detection by screening and when the cancer would have been diagnosed from symptoms. Survival counted from diagnosis grows by that interval even when the date of death is unchanged, which matches better survival with no change in mortality. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 17; 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 19; 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 21; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 10.5"},{"q":"Researchers in Denmark looked at the incidence of breast cancer over 3 decades in areas of the country where mammogram screening was introduced compared to locations where screening was not offered. Small invasive cancers (<2 cm) were found more frequently in the screened group, but the incidence of large invasive cancers (>2 cm) was similar for the screened and unscreened groups. These findings remained after controlling for age differences. Which of the following is true?","choices":["If lead-time bias is present, both cancer survival rates and cancer mortality rates will be lower in the screening group.","Many of the smaller tumors detected may not have become clinically significant","Overdiagnosis is not present because the cancers found were invasive.","The study does not account for possible changes in the cancer incidence over time."],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. More small invasive cancers in screened areas with no change in large invasive cancers suggests overdiagnosis: some small tumors detected would never have become clinically significant. Finding disease that does not help the patient is the overdiagnosis problem. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 23; 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 17; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 10.7"},{"q":"A review compared the performance of HPV testing versus cytology (pap smears) for cervical cancer screening. Compared to cytology, HPV testing had better detection of precancerous lesions that would be treated to prevent subsequent cancers. However, it also had a higher false-positive rate. Which of the following is incorrect?","choices":["HPV is more sensitive than cytology for screening.","HPV is less specific than cytology for screening.","More frequent testing may improve the sensitivity of cytology.","The positive predictive value is higher for HPV than cytology."],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, this is the incorrect statement. Positive predictive value is true positives divided by all positives. HPV testing adds true positives through higher sensitivity but adds more false positives through lower specificity, and the extra false positives outweigh the gain, so at a given prevalence its positive predictive value is lower than for cytology, not higher. Its higher sensitivity gives it the better negative predictive value. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 24; 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 27; 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 21; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 10.8"},{"q":"When assessing a new vaccine, which of the following is least important:","choices":["Efficacy in preventing the disease","Safety of the vaccine","Danger of the disease","Cost of giving the vaccine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Cost matters, but it is only one component of a cost-effectiveness analysis, which also depends on efficacy, safety and the burden of the disease. Of the four considerations it is the least important on its own. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 7; 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 9; 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 27; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 10.9"},{"q":"When the same test is used in diagnostic and screening situations, which of the following statements is most likely correct?","choices":["The sensitivity and specificity will likely be the same in both situations.","The positive predictive value will be higher in a screening situation.","Overdiagnosis is equally likely in both situations.","Disease prevalence will be higher in the diagnostic situation."],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Diagnostic tests are used in symptomatic people, so disease prevalence is higher than among asymptomatic screened people. Higher prevalence raises positive predictive value and lowers negative predictive value. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 7; 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 27; 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 28; 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 22; 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 23; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 10.10"},{"q":"For a cost-effectiveness analysis of a preventive activity, which kinds of costs should be included?","choices":["Medical costs, such as those associated with delivering the preventive intervention","All medical costs, including diagnostic follow-up of positive tests and treatment for persons diagnosed with disease, with and without the preventive activity","Indirect costs, such as loss of income due to time off from work, among patients receiving the prevention and those who develop the disease","Indirect costs for both patients and care givers","All of the above"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. A cost-effectiveness analysis takes a societal perspective and captures all costs: delivery of the preventive activity, diagnostic follow-up and treatment (with and without prevention), and indirect costs for patients and caregivers. Costs without prevention are subtracted from costs with prevention to estimate the cost per health effect. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 10.13"}]};
